<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="S4.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Effect of per-head register count <math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and over-segmentation.</span> M2O vs. O2O mIoU (%) on RATS-S. Higher M2O reflects better clustering; a large M2O–O2O gap indicates over-segmentation. <math id="S4.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}16</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the best M2O, while <math id="S4.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> trades absolute M2O for higher O2O on part-centric classes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> region ablation.</span>
At inference, we rank registers by activation magnitude and select the top-<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to form the segmentation map. Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.5 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ RATS! Patches Talk Through Registers:Emergent Parts in Register Attention Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reveals a coverage-purity trade-off: increasing <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> from 10 to 80 improves mIoU by up to 9.2% on IN-S919, but ARI consistently decreases (e.g., <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.9% on IN-S919), since lower-ranked registers attend to less coherent areas. The metrics saturate beyond <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{\approx}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{\approx}50</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting a natural ranking among visual primitives learned by the bottleneck.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
